{
  "id": "PSI-101",
  "summary_plain": "The SAME-R investigation burned solid materials like Kapton, Pyrell, Teflon, lampwick, and silicone on the International Space Station to study how smoke and liquid aerosol particles form in microgravity. Without normal gravity currents, the smoke particles can grow larger and change shape, which affects how spacecraft smoke detectors work. Understanding these smoke properties helps engineers design better and more reliable fire detection systems for future space missions.",
  "summary_expert": "The SAME-R investigation evaluated smoke aerosol generation using solid fuels including Kapton, Pyrell, Teflon, lampwick, and silicone rubber heated below their ignition points in microgravity on the ISS. The experiments varied the forced air flow speeds from 0 to 8.1 cm/s, altered sample heating temperatures, and adjusted primary aging times from 11 to 1800 s. Diagnostics measured particle counts, mass concentrations, and size moments, determining that the majority of average smoke particle sizes fell in the 200 nm to 400 nm range, with zero-flow tests producing particle diameters of average mass 30 % to 40 % larger compared to flow tests.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "text": "The diameter of average mass is 30 % to 40 % larger for the zero-flow tests compared to the flow tests.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-101",
        "doi": "10.60555/2np7-jv14",
        "file": "documents/PSI-101/PSI-101_Reports_SAME-R_Final Report.pdf",
        "page": 9
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "The morphology of the Pyrell smoke changed from fractal-like clusters with up to 30 primary spheres for the case with a flow of 8 cm/s to spherical particles for the zero-flow condition.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-101",
        "doi": "10.60555/2np7-jv14",
        "file": "documents/PSI-101/PSI-101_Reports_SAME-R_Final Report.pdf",
        "page": 9
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "The majority of the average smoke particle sizes were found to be in the 200 nm to 400 nm range with the quiescent cases producing some cases with substantially larger particles.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-101",
        "doi": "10.60555/2np7-jv14",
        "file": "documents/PSI-101/PSI-101_Reports_SAME-R_Final Report.pdf",
        "page": 72
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "For typical runs, the particle concentrations were a few million particles / cc and the mass concentrations were 2 to 15 mg/m3.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-101",
        "doi": "10.60555/2np7-jv14",
        "file": "documents/PSI-101/PSI-101_Reports_SAME-R_Final Report.pdf",
        "page": 4
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    }
